## ClientAccessPolicy.xml (Legacy Silverlight Cross-Domain Policy)

### Description

`clientaccesspolicy.xml` is a legacy XML file used by Microsoft Silverlight to control whether applications hosted on one domain can access resources on another domain. The file is typically located at:

```text
https://example.com/clientaccesspolicy.xml
```

Although Silverlight is now deprecated, some legacy applications still expose this file. If it is overly permissive, it may allow unauthorized cross-domain access.

---

## Dangerous Configuration Example

```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<access-policy>
  <cross-domain-access>
    <policy>
      <allow-from http-request-headers="*">
        <domain uri="*"/>
      </allow-from>
      <grant-to>
        <resource path="/" include-subpaths="true"/>
      </grant-to>
    </policy>
  </cross-domain-access>
</access-policy>
```

---

## Why is this dangerous?

### 1. `<domain uri="*"/>`

* Allows **any domain** on the Internet to access the application.

Example:

* `https://evil.com`
* `https://attacker.com`
* `https://randomsite.com`

All are trusted.

---

### 2. `http-request-headers="*"`

Allows clients to send **any HTTP headers**, including:

```
Authorization
Cookie
Content-Type
X-API-Key
Custom Headers
```

This gives unnecessary flexibility to untrusted domains.

---

### 3. `path="/"`

Grants access starting from the website root.

Example:

```
/
/api/
/admin/
/users/
/files/
```

---

### 4. `include-subpaths="true"`

Allows access to every subdirectory under the root.

Example:

```
/
/api/
/api/v1/
/admin/
/uploads/
/private/
```

Everything becomes accessible if Silverlight is used.


